<p>Nigeria’s military says about 100 U.S. troops have arrived in the country for a training and support mission. The announcement comes as Nigeria faces a surge in attacks by extremist and other armed groups in parts of the country.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Gr4Jl0RLLqpKsnMLAFX75FyCE6NRJS1wS93-xVMrs1BYX60pNuqVH9zEjcY-q95eoRf9bN4QdBEwQ3BdInRLrGntXe41ZHGo_QL-rgzJBintQV3qekAQU3yRApDP-iMpk-x2BmZo22DgDfmkH4UJF0ntcnKiMIFm9Htpo5qkrR4VunJXK_vCSzO9DuFGrvPf3B748pjXkDg3RTKR8NSKHMjpyKk' alt='Photo illustrating reporting on U.S. troop arrival for a training mission in Nigeria.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Mission limits: Nigerian officials said the U.S. soldiers will not take on a combat role and will operate under Nigeria’s full command authority.</li><li>What Nigeria asked for: Nigeria told Washington it wanted training, technical support, and intelligence-sharing to help counter violent groups.</li><li>Arrival details: Defence Headquarters spokesperson Samaila Uba said the troops and associated equipment landed at Bauchi Airfield.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Nigeria Defence Headquarters: Military spokesperson Samaila Uba said in a Monday evening statement that U.S. personnel arrived with associated equipment. (<a href='https://allafrica.com/stories/202602170095.html'>AllAfrica (Premium Times)</a>)</li><li>Straits Times/Reuters framing: The report cast the arrival as part of Washington scaling up an operation focused on Islamist insurgents. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/nigeria-says-100-more-u-s-military-personnel-arrive-to-tackle-islamists'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>The Hindu reporting: The defence ministry did not say exactly when the “U.S. military trainers” arrived or when they were due to arrive. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/nigeria-announces-arrival-of-100-us-soldiers/article70640696.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://allafrica.com/stories/202602170095.html'>N